Main Modules & When They Are Used

OpenCV is structured into modular shared/static libraries. Its core main modules include: 

• core: Basic data structures (such as Mat for multi-dimensional arrays) and fundamental mathematical/pixel operations. Used in almost every OpenCV program.
• imgproc: Image processing functions including filtering, geometric transformations, color space conversions (e.g., BGR to Grayscale or HSV), blurring, and thresholding. Used for pre-processing images before analysis.
• imgcodecs: Image reading and writing functions like imread() and imwrite(). Used to load images from disk or save processed results.
• videoio: Video capture and video stream input/output (e.g., reading from video files or live webcams via VideoCapture). Used for handling live video feeds or video files.
• highgui: Graphical user interface tools to create windows, display images (imshow()), and handle mouse or keyboard events. Used for simple visual inspection and interactive displays.
• video: Motion analysis and object tracking algorithms (e.g., optical flow, background subtraction). Used in surveillance, tracking, and motion detection.
• calib3d: Camera calibration, stereo correspondence, and 3D reconstruction. Used in robotics, augmented reality, and 3D mapping.
• features2d: Feature detection and description (e.g., SIFT, ORB) to find and match keypoints between images. Used in image stitching, object recognition, and SLAM.
• objdetect: Pre-trained object and face detectors (e.g., Haar cascades, HOG). Used for face detection and pedestrian tracking.
• dnn: Deep Neural Networks module for running pre-trained deep learning models (from PyTorch, TensorFlow, Keras). Used for modern AI-based image classification, segmentation, and detection.
• ml: Classical machine learning algorithms (Support Vector Machines, K-Nearest Neighbors, decision trees). Used for standard statistical classification and regression tasks. [1] (https://docs.opencv.org/4.13.0/), [2] (https://www.opencv.org.cn/opencvdoc/2.3.1/opencv2refman.pdf), [3] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PeMM80WimN4), [4] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LWnGRiLf70s&t=147), [5] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oXlwWbU8l2o&t=254), [6] (https://data-flair.training/blogs/introduction-to-opencv/), [7] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMqH2fYhxh0), [8] (https://learnopencv.com/getting-started-with-opencv/), [9] (https://roboflow.com/blog/what-is-opencv), [10] (https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/opencv-python-tutorial/)


Advanced & Specialized OpenCV Modules
Beyond the standard core modules, OpenCV includes a highly specialized layer of modules designed for complex computer vision tasks. These are crucial for building specialized industrial applications or conducting deep AI research:
• photo (Computational Photography): Functions for advanced image manipulation such as non-photorealistic rendering, high dynamic range (HDR) imaging, image inpainting (restoring damaged parts of a photo), and advanced denoising.
• shape (Shape Distance & Matching): Contains algorithms for structural shape extraction, shape descriptors, and measuring the distance/similarity between contours. Used heavily in optical character recognition (OCR) and traditional shape-based classification.
• superres (Super Resolution): Advanced algorithms used to upscale lower-resolution images and video frames to higher resolutions by leveraging spatial and temporal data. Used in modern security forensics and media restoration.
• videostab (Video Stabilization): Tools designed to eliminate camera shake and jitter from raw video recordings by calculating camera path metrics and warping frames to appear smooth. Used in dashcam software, drone tracking, and action cameras.
• viz (3D Visualizer): A 3D visualization window framework based on VTK (Visualization Toolkit). It provides an easy way to render 3D points, point clouds, coordinate systems, meshes, and shapes. Used extensively to visualize output from LiDAR sensors or 3D camera reconstructions.

